-\cvnamdonly{\cvsubsubsec{Restarting in NAMD.}{}
-Before the Colvars module is initialized in NAMD, the \texttt{colvarsInput} keyword can be used to give the name of a state file.
-After initialization of the Colvars module, a state file may be loaded at any time with the Tcl command \texttt{cv load}.
-Both versions support loading Colvars state files in either format (binary or text).
+\cvnamdonly{\cvsubsubsec{Restarting in NAMD.}{sec:colvars_input_namd}
+When the initial configuration is provided using the NAMD keyword \texttt{colvarsConfig}, the \texttt{colvarsInput} NAMD keyword can be used to give the name of a state file, which will be read immediately at startup.
+On the other hand, for loading an input state file after startup, the Tcl command \texttt{cv load} (see \ref{sec:cv_scripting} and \ref{sec:cv_command_loadsave}) is also available.
+Note that the two approaches \emph{are not interchangeable}: because the \texttt{colvarsConfig} and \texttt{colvarsInput} keywords are processed at NAMD startup, their effect \emph{precedes} any command included in the Tcl-scripted portion of a NAMD simulation script, including \texttt{cv load}.
+
+To ensure that the Colvars configuration is always loaded before an input state file is read, one should \emph{either}:
+\begin{itemize}
+\item use the keywords \texttt{colvarsConfig} and \texttt{colvarsInput} if the Colvars configuration never changes during the NAMD run, \emph{or}
+\item use the Tcl commands \texttt{cv config} (or \texttt{cv configfile}) and \texttt{cv load} to manage Colvars from the Tcl-scripted portion of a NAMD input script.
+\end{itemize}
+
+The \texttt{colvarsInput} keyword and the \texttt{cv load} command both support loading Colvars state files in either binary or text formats.
 } % end \cvnamdonly
 
 
